{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read framework:unirank/pytorch/dataloaders/unirank_dataloader.py --find 'history_mask' --lines 90\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 307 --lines 75\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve paper:hiformer\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-method-est\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-method-hiformer\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-method-rankmixer\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": null,\n  \"status\": \"no matching text\",\n  \"total_lines\": 598\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 307,\n  \"next_line\": 382,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\\n330: In the training phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is the mean and variance of input in each mini-batch.\\n331: In the testing phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is calculated by moving averages E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] over data.\\n332: ϵ\\\\epsilon is a small constant which is set to be 10−810^{-8} in our practice.\\n333: Dice can be viewed as a generalization of PReLu.\\n334: The key idea of Dice is to adaptively adjust the rectified point according to distribution of input data, whose value is set to be the mean of input.\\n335: Besides, Dice controls smoothly to switch between the two channels.\\n336: When E⁡(s)=0E(s)=0 and V​a​r​[s]=0Var[s]=0, Dice degenerates into PReLU.\\n337: 6. Experiments\\n338: In this section, we present our experiments in detail, including datasets, evaluation metric, experimental setup, model comparison and the corresponding analysis.\\n339: Experiments on two public datasets with user behaviors as well as a dataset collected from the display advertising system in Alibaba demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approach which outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the CTR prediction task.\\n340: Both the public datasets and experiment codes are made available1.\\n341: 6.1. Datasets and Experimental Setup\\n342: Amazon Dataset22\\n343: 2\\n344: http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/.\\n345: Amazon Dataset contains product reviews and metadata from Amazon, which is used as benchmark dataset(He and McAuley, 2016; Mcauley\\n346: et al., ; Veit\\n347: et al., 2015). We conduct experiments on a subset named Electronics, which contains 192,403 users, 63,001 goods, 801 categories and 1,689,188 samples. User behaviors in this dataset are rich, with more than 5 reviews for each users and goods. Features include goods_id, cate_id, user reviewed goods_id_list and cate_id_list. Let all behaviors of a user be (b1,b2,…,bk,…,bnb_{1},b_{2},\\\\ldots,b_{k},\\\\ldots,b_{n}), the task is to predict the (k+1)-th reviewed goods by making use of the first k reviewed goods.\\n348: Training dataset is generated with k=1,2,…,n−2k=1,2,\\\\ldots,n-2 for each user. In the test set, we predict the last one given the first n−1n-1 reviewed goods.\\n349: For all models, we use SGD as the optimizer with exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 1 and decay rate is set to 0.1. The mini-batch size is set to be 3232.\\n350: MovieLens Dataset33\\n351: 3\\n352: https://grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/20m/. MovieLens data(Harper and\\n353: Konstan, 2015) contains 138,493 users, 27,278 movies, 21 categories and 20,000,263 samples. To make it suitable for CTR prediction task, we transform it into a binary classification data. Original user rating of the movies is continuous value ranging from 0 to 5. We label the samples with rating of 4 and 5 to be positive and the rest to be negative. We segment the data into training and testing dataset based on userID. Among all 138,493 users, of which 100,000 are randomly selected into training set (about 14,470,000 samples) and the rest 38,493 into the test set (about 5,530,000 samples). The task is to predict whether user will rate a given movie to be above 3(positive label) based on historical behaviors. Features include movie_id, movie_cate_id and user rated movie_id_list, movie_cate_id_list. We use the same optimizer, learning rate and mini-batch size as described on Amazon Dataset.\\n354: Alibaba Dataset. We collected traffic logs from the online display advertising system in Alibaba, of which two weeks’ samples are used for training and samples of the following day for testing. The size of training and testing set is about 2 billions and 0.14 billion respectively. For all the deep models, the dimensionality of embedding vector is 12 for the whole 16 groups of features. Layers of MLP is set by 192×200×80×2192\\\\times 200\\\\times 80\\\\times 2. Due to the huge size of data, we set the mini-batch size to be 5000 and use Adam(Kingma and Ba, 2015) as the optimizer. We apply exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 0.001 and decay rate is set to 0.9.\\n355: The statistics of all the above datasets is shown in Table 2.\\n356: Volume of Alibaba Dataset is much larger than both Amazon and MovieLens, which brings more challenges.\\n357: Table 2. Statistics of datasets used in this paper.\\n358: Dataset\\n359: Users\\n360: Goodsa\\n361: Categories\\n362: Samples\\n363: Amazon(Electro).\\n364: 192,403\\n365: 63,001\\n366: 801\\n367: 1,689,188\\n368: MovieLens.\\n369: 138,493\\n370: 27,278\\n371: 21\\n372: 20,000,263\\n373: Alibaba.\\n374: 60 million\\n375: 0.6 billion\\n376: 100,000\\n377: 2.14 billion\\n378: a\\n379: For MovieLens dataset, goods refer to be movies.\\n380: Figure 4. Performances of BaseModel with different regularizations on Alibaba Dataset. Training with fine-grained g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​sgoods\\\\_ids features without regularization encounters serious overfitting after the first epoch. All the regularizations show improvement, among which our proposed mini-batch aware regularization performs best. Besides, well trained model with g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​sgoods\\\\_ids features gets higher AUC than without them. It comes from the richer information that fine-grained features contained.\\n381: 6.2. Competitors\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:hiformer\",\n  \"title\": \"HiFormer: Heterogeneous Feature Interactions Learning with Transformers for Recommender Systems\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.05884\",\n  \"version\": \"original paper link recorded at UniRank git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"kind\": \"original_method_paper\",\n  \"content_read\": false,\n  \"status\": \"Original paper link verified in pinned README; full original paper not audited in this initialization.\",\n  \"fallback_ref\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Link and provenance only. Read original primary paper before a stronger novelty or reproduction claim.\",\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-method-est\",\n  \"knowledge_type\": \"industry\",\n  \"scheme\": \"EST\",\n  \"origin\": \"model compared in UniRank; industrial/academic method reference, not a competition award\",\n  \"family\": \"layer_wise\",\n  \"sourc